tasks.description:
Under `// Task 1` in `src/caddy.h`, write a class template called `Caddy`, with one type `T`. It keeps its items in a private `std::vector<T>` called `items` and its limit in a private `int` called `limit`. Its public parts are:

- a constructor that takes the limit as an `int`, and starts with no items;
- `add`, which takes an item as a reference to a `const T`. When the caddy holds fewer items than its limit, it puts a copy of the item at the end and gives back `true`; when it is full, it changes nothing and gives back `false`;
- `count`, which changes nothing and gives back how many items it holds, as an `int`;
- the declaration of `at`, which takes a place as an `int`, changes nothing and gives back a reference to a `const T`. Its body is Task 2's.

The bodies of the constructor, `add` and `count` go inside the class. A type `T` needs to offer only copies for a `Caddy` to work.

Check builds a program of its own that includes `caddy.h`. It makes a `Caddy<int>` and a `Caddy` of a type of its own that can be copied and offers nothing else, adds items past the limit, and reads `count` through a `const Caddy&`. It also compiles two lines of its own outside the class that read `items` and `limit`, which must be refused.
